        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ஆத்திரம்",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ஆத்திரம் — āttiram — \"rage; fury\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "அவன் ஆத்திரத்தில் கத்தினான். avaṉ āttirattil kattiṉāṉ. He shouted in a rage."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A -அம் noun takes the oblique -அத்து- before an ending: ஆத்திரத்தில், in a rage."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The origin of ஆத்திரம் (āttiram) is not settled. It is often set beside Sanskrit ātura, \"suffering, distressed\" (Monier-Williams), but the link is uncertain."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing what you lived and felt."
            }
